Abstract
Pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs) are the main source of extracellular matrix proteins in pancreatic fibrosis, a pathological feature of chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer. Interferon- $\gamma(\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma)$ is an antifibrotic cytokine, but how precisely it exerts its effects on PSCs is largely unknown. Here, we have focussed on the role of STAT1 as well as target genes of $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ signalling. Our data indicate that $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ regulates the expression of two autocrine mediators of PSC activation, connective tissue growth factor and endothelin-1, in a transforming growth factor- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ -antagonistic manner. STAT1 overexpression under the control of a tetracycline-dependent promoter revealed a close correlation between STAT1 expression and activation, the biological effects of $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ (growth inhibition, induction of apoptosis), and target gene expression. Our data further support the hypothesis that $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ interferes with stellate cell activation in the pancreas and suggest activated STAT1 as an inductor of a quiescent PSC phenotype.
