## 1. Introduction
In pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer, extensive deposition of extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins, leading to organ fibrosis, is a constant pathological finding and considered as an aggravating factor of both diseases. Specific anti-fibrotic therapies are not available yet.
Recent studies have identified fibroblast-like cells, termed pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs) [1,2], as the main source of ECM proteins in the diseased organ. They are closely related with hepatic stellate cells (HSCs), the key effector cells in liver fibrosis [3]. In response to pro-fibrogenic stimuli, PSCs proliferate and undergo typical morphological as well as functional changes, including a loss of retinoid-containing fat droplets, expression of the myofibroblastic marker $\upalpha$ -smooth muscle actin ( $\mathrm{{\dot{\alpha}}}$ -SMA), and enhanced synthesis of ECM components [4,5]. A similar process, considered as PSC activation, is induced when isolated PSCs are plated on cell culture dishes [1,2,6–8]. To overcome limitations caused by the low availability of PSCs, we have recently generated immortalised PSC lines (termed LTCs) through SV40 large T-antigen transfer [9].
In the past few years, the molecular principles of PSC activation have become a focus of attention, and both extracellular regulators and intracellular mediators of activation signals have been studied. Thus, it has been shown that platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) exerts strong mitogenic effects on PSCs, while transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β) has been suggested as the most potent stimulator of ECM synthesis [10,11]. In addition, ethanol and its metabolites as well as oxidative stress signals have been implicated in the activation process [8]. We and others have shown that mitogen-activated protein (MAP) kinases are key mediators of activation signals [12–16], while activation of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma $({\mathrm{PPAR}}\gamma)$ , a key transcription factor for adipocyte differentiation [17], is associated with the exhibition of a quiescent phenotype [18–20].
Recently, we have provided evidence that interferons (IFNs) exert direct inhibitory effects on pancreatic stellate cells, reducing both cell proliferation and collagen synthesis in vitro [21]. The finding is of potential clinical interest because IFNs are available for therapeutic purposes. Furthermore, they have already proven useful in the treatment of patients with chronic hepatitis B or C; diseases which are associated with liver fibrosis [22,23]. Interestingly, the efficiency of different IFNs to inhibit PSC growth and ECM production was closely correlated with their ability to activate STAT (signal transducer and activator of transcription) 1, with IFN- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}$ showing only weak and $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ much stronger effects [21].
In the transduction of signals from activated IFN-receptors to the nucleus, STAT transcription factors, activated by tyrosine kinases of the Janus family (JAKs), play a key role [24,25]. STAT1 contributes to IFN signalling by forming homodimers as well as, in response to IFN- $\scriptstyle\cdot\alpha,$ /IFN- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\beta}}$ stimulation, the ISGF3 complex that also contains STAT2 and the IFN regulatory family protein p48 [26]. The precise role of STAT proteins in the regulation of stellate cell function has not been characterised so far.
In this study, we have further analysed how $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ exerts its biological effects on PSCs and focussed on the role of STAT1 as well as target genes of $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ signalling. The results of overexpression studies suggest STAT1 as an essential mediator of the growth-inhibitory and pro-apoptotic effects of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ in stellate cells. Furthermore, quantitative gene expression analysis revealed a direct antagonism between $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ and TGF-β1 with respect to the control of connective tissue growth factor (CTGF) and endothelin-1 (ET-1) expression; two molecules that have been implicated in fibrogenesis and the PSC activation process [27,28].
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Material
Iscove's modified Dulbecco's medium (IMDM) and supplements for cell culture were obtained from Biochrom (Berlin, Germany). The RNeasy kit was from Qiagen (Hilden, Germany), T7- $\left(\mathrm{dT}\right)_{24}$ primer, superscript II-reverse transcriptase and the Topo TA Cloning kit from Invitrogen (Karlsruhe, Germany), and pTRE2purHA, pTet-off, puromycin, as well as propidium iodide from BD Biosciences (Heidelberg, Germany). The enhanced chemoluminescence (ECL) Plus kit was purchased from Amersham Biosciences (Freiburg, Germany), the phospho-STAT1 antibody and restriction enzymes from New England BioLabs (Frankfurt, Germany), and anti-STAT1 protein antibody from Santa Cruz Biotechnologies (Santa Cruz, CA, USA). Tetracycline (tet) and the $\upalpha$ -SMA antibody were delivered by Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO, USA), oligonucleotides by BioTeZ (Berlin, Germany), and R-phycoerythrinstreptavidin by MoBiTec (Göttingen, Germany). Gene Chip™ Rat Genome 230 2.0 Arrays as well as the GeneChip™ Expression $3^{\prime}$ Amplification One-Cycle Target Labeling and Control Reagents Kit were obtained from Affymetrix (Santa Clara, CA, USA). Taqman™ reagents were purchased from Applied Biosystems (Foster City, CA, USA), TransFast™ Transfection Reagent and the plasmids PGL3-Basic as well as pRL-CMV from Promega, and recombinant cytokines (rat $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ , human TGF-β1) from R and D Systems (Minneapolis, MN, USA).
## 2.2. Cell culture
Stellate cell lines were cultured in IMDM supplemented with $10\%$ fetal calf serum (FCS), non-essential amino acids, $100\ \mathrm{U/ml}$ penicillin and $100~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ streptomycin at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ humidified atmosphere. Tet was applied as described below.
## 2.3. Cloning, subcloning and expression of STAT1 alpha
To clone the rat STAT1α cDNA, RNA was isolated from PSC with the RNeasy kit. $1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of total RNA was reverse transcribed into cDNA as described [20]. The rat STAT1α cDNA (positions 1-2250, comprising the complete open reading frame; GenBank Accession Number AF205604) was amplified by PCR. To facilitate subcloning, upstream and downstream primers also carried a Mlu I and a Cla I site, respectively (upstream primer: ${\boldsymbol{5}}^{\prime}$ -TCA TTG CTA GCG ATG GTT GAC ACA GAG ATG CC- $3^{\prime}$ , downstream primer $5^{\prime}$ -TGA ATA GCT AGC AGC GAC TGG GAC TTT TCT GC-3′). The PCR product was ligated into pCR 2.1-Topo (Invitrogen) using the Topo TA Cloning kit.
For tet-dependent expression, the STAT1α cDNA was subcloned into pTRE2purHA (confers puromycin resistance), and the correct sequence of the inserts was verified by didesoxy sequencing of the complete coding regions. The resulting constructs encodes for a STAT1α protein with an N-terminal hemagglutinin (HA)-tag, expression of which is controlled by a tet response element.
For STAT1 overexpression studies, we used LTC-Tet cells [9] that stably express the transcriptional activator protein tTA, which is inactivated upon tet binding. LTC-Tet cells were transfected with the pTRE2purHA-STAT construct by lipofection. Therefore, cells growing at a low density $(5\%$ confluency) in 6-well plates were incubated with plasmid DNA $2~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ per well), using TransFast™ Transfection Reagent according to the vendor's specifications. After transfection, the cells were cultured in medium supplemented with tet $(2~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , before puromycin was added at $2~\mathrm{\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}}$ . Upgrowing resistant cells were tested for a tet-dependent STAT1α expression profile, and clones with a strong induction of STAT1α transgene expression after tet-withdrawal were selected. Unless otherwise stated, puromycinresistant cells (referred to as LTC-STAT1 cells) were permanently cultured with tet $(2~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ .
## 2.4. Cell proliferation assay
Effects of STAT1α overexpression on cell proliferation were assessed using the 5-bromo- $\cdot2^{\prime}$ -deoxyuridine (BrdU) labelling and detection enzymelinked immunosorbent assay kit (Roche Diagnostics; Mannheim, Germany). Therefore, the cells were grown in 96-well plates in the presence or absence of tet, FCS and cytokines (TGF-β1, IFN-γ) as indicated. After $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ of labelling, BrdU uptake was measured according to the manufacturer's instructions.
## 2.5. Analysis of cellular DNA content by flow cytometry (sub-G1-peak)
Cells were grown to about $80\%$ confluency and then incubated in complete culture medium with or without tet and $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ as indicated. Afterwards, they were harvested with trypsin-EDTA to obtain a single cell suspension. Cells were pelleted by centrifugation, washed twice with PBS and resuspended in $2\mathrm{ml}$ icecold $70\%$ ethanol for at least $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $-20{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Next, cells were pelleted again, washed twice with PBS and incubated for one hour in $400~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ PBS containing $0.1\%$ Tween 20 and $\mathrm{1~mg/ml}$ RNase A (Roche Diagnostics) at $4~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After the addition of $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ propidium iodide $\mathrm{10^{6}}$ cells, samples were subjected to cytofluorometric analysis using a FACScan (Becton Dickinson) and the Cellquest program. 10.000 events were measured for each sample and the data stored in list-mode for further analysis.
## 2.6. Immunoblotting
Protein extracts of stellate cells were prepared, adjusted to identical protein concentrations and subjected to immunoblot analysis as previously described [12]. The blots were developed using ECL Plus.
## 2.7. Microarray analysis of RNA expression profiles
To analyse gene expression changes in response to $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ and TGF-β1 stimulation, Affymetrix microarray technology was employed. Therefore, total RNA from LTC-Tet cells pretreated as indicated was extracted using the RNeasy kit. High density oligonucleotide array hybridisation was performed essentially as described before [20]. Briefly, first strand cDNA synthesis was carried out by superscript II reverse transcriptase and T7- $\cdot(\mathrm{dT})_{24}$ primer using $5~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of RNA. Second strand synthesis was performed according to the GeneChip™ Expression $3^{\prime}$ Amplification One-Cycle Target Labeling Kit followed by an in vitro transcription reaction in order to label the cRNA samples with biotin-16- UTP. After chip hybridisation (overnight at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ), staining was performed with R-phycoerythrin streptavidin followed by an antibody amplification procedure using a biotinylated anti-streptavidin antibody and goat $\mathrm{IgG}$ . The fluorescence intensity was scanned, and data were scaled based on total intensity with the Affymetrix GeneChip software (GeneChip™ Operating Software (GCOS 1.2), Data Mining Tool 3.1).
Gene expression was analysed by means of the Gene Chip™ Rat Genome 230 2.0 Array interrogating over 28.000 well-substantiated rat genes. Affymetrix software was used for the first level of data analysis, including the “present” or “absent” call, and pairwise comparisons based on the onesided Wilcoxon's rank sum test. Further analysis was performed as described in the table legends.
## 2.8. Quantitative reverse transcriptase-PCR using real-time TaqMan™ technology
Total RNA from stellate cells pretreated as indicated was isolated with the RNeasy kit, and $1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of RNA was reverse transcribed into cDNA by means of TaqMan™ Reverse Transcription Reagents and random hexamer priming. Target cDNA levels were analysed by quantitative real-time PCR using TaqMan™ Universal PCR Master Mix and the following Assay-on-Demand™ rat gene-specific fluorescently labelled TaqMan™ MGB probes in an ABI Prism 7000 sequence detection system (Applied Biosystems): Rn00573960_g1 (CTGF), $00561129_{-}\mathrm{m}1$ (ET-1), Rn 00595838_s1 (SOCS-1), and Rn01527838_g1 (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase [HPRT]; used as a house-keeping gene control). PCR conditions were as follows: $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ , 50 cycles of $15\mathrm{~s~}$ at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/1$ min at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . PCR reactions were performed in triplicate. The relative expression of each mRNA compared with HPRT was calculated according to the equation $\Delta C\mathrm{t}{=}\mathrm{Ct}_{\mathrm{target}}{-}\mathrm{Ct}_{\mathrm{HPRT}}{.}$ The relative amount of target mRNA in control cells (defined in the legend to Fig. 5) and cells treated by tet withdrawal and $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ stimulation as indicated was expressed as $2^{-(\Delta\Delta\dot{\mathrm{Ct}})}$ , where $\Delta\Delta\mathrm{Ct}_{\mathrm{treatment}}{=}\Delta\mathrm{Ct}_{\mathrm{treatment}}{-}\Delta\mathrm{Ct}_{\mathrm{control}}$ .
## 2.9. Analysis of CTGF promoter activity
A 875 bp fragment of the human CTGF promoter (GenBank Accession Number AF316368, positions $-742$ to $+133$ with the transcription initiation site as 1) was amplified by PCR from genomic DNA (source: cell line MiaPaCa) as described by Zhao et al. [29]. The resulting PCR product was ligated into pCR 2.1-Topo using the Topo TA Cloning kit and verified by didesoxy sequencing. Subsequently, the fragment was subcloned into the plasmid PGL3-Basic upstream of the firefly luciferase (Luc) reporter gene, generating the reporter vector PGL3-CTGF.
CTGF promoter activity was analysed in transient transfection assays, using the Dual-Luciferase reporter assay system (Promega). In this system, the activities of the experimental firefly luciferase and a constitutively expressed renilla luciferase (encoded by a co-transfected plasmid) can be measured sequentially from a single sample, allowing the normalisation of transfection efficiencies. Therefore, subconfluent cultures of stellate cells growing in 96-well plates were transfected with $200~\mathrm{ng}$ of the indicated PGL3-vector and $10~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of the renilla luciferase vector pRL-CMV, using TransFast™ Transfection Reagent according to the manufacturer´s instructions. Transfected cells were treated with cytokines for $24\mathrm{h}$ as indicated. Afterwards, luciferase activities in the attached cells were measured as suggested by the vendor, and ratio's of firefly and renilla luciferase activities were determined. For each independent transfection, data were expressed as percent of corresponding untreated controls.
## 2.10. Statistical analysis
Results are expressed as means $\cdot\pm$ standard error of the mean (SEM) for the indicated number of independent cultures per experimental protocol. Statistical significance was checked using Wilcoxon's rank sum test. $p{<}0.05$ was considered to be statistically significant.
## 3. Results
In an initial series of experiments, we employed microarray technology to analyse global changes of gene expression in $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ treated stellate cells, using the immortalised cell clone LTC-Tet as a model. To gain further insight into the transcriptional regulation of PSC function, effects of the antifibrotic cytokine $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ were directly compared with the effects of a pro-fibrogenic mediator, TGF-β1. Table 1 depicts those genes that are up-regulated by $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma_{;}$ , but down-regulated by TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ . At the top of the list, many gene products are found that have previously been implicated in $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ action in other types of cells, including Mx proteins, the antigenic peptide transporters tap1 and tap2, oligoadenylate synthetase, interferon regulatory factor-1 (IRF1), caspases 1 and 11, and chemokines such as Cxcl10. The data also confirm up-regulation of interleukin (IL)-15 by $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ , as it has previously been reported by our group [30].
Although the precise function of most of these proteins in stellate cells remains to be deciphered, our attention was particularly attracted by the transcription factor STAT1 (no. 8 in Table 1): As described above, we previously found a close correlation between the efficiency of interferons to induce STAT1 phosphorylation, and to inhibit PSC activation [21]. To study the involvement of STAT1 in the regulation of PSC function more directly, the isoform $\operatorname{STAT1}\upalpha$ was overexpressed under the control of a tet-dependent promoter. LTC-Tet cells, which stably express the tTA protein [20], were transfected with pTRE2purHA-STAT vector as described above.
LTC-STAT1 cells cultured with tet at $2~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ expressed the endogenous STAT1 protein only (Fig. 1A, lane 7), suggesting suppression of transgene transcription. Withdrawal of tet induced a strong expression of the transgenic protein, which migrated slower because of its HA-tag (Fig. 1A, lanes 1–6). For maximal STAT1α transgene expression, $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ of tet-free culture was sufficient (Fig. 1A, lane 3). Analysis of STAT1 activation (Fig. 1B) revealed appropriate tyrosine phosphorylation of both the endogenous and the transgenic $\operatorname{STAT1}\alpha$ in response to IFN$\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ stimulation (upper panel, lanes 2 and 4).
Next, we studied the growth characteristics of LTC-STAT1 cells under different experimental conditions. Tet withdrawal alone, without additional cytokine treatment, caused a reduction of cell growth by approximately $30\%$ (Fig. 2A). IFN- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\gamma}$ inhibited proliferation of LTC-STAT1 cells both in the presence and absence of tet in a dose-dependent manner (Fig. 2B). However, after induction of transgene expression by tet-withdrawal the antiproliferative effect of $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ at two different concentrations (10 and $100~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}$ , respectively) was significantly enhanced. In contrast, TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ $(5~\mathrm{ng/ml})$ reduced the growth rate of cells cultured with and without tet in a similar manner. As expected, TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ had also no effect on STAT1 tyrosine phosphorylation and activation (data not shown).
Further studies revealed that $\mathrm{STAT1}\upalpha$ overexpressing cells were also more susceptible to $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma.$ -induced apoptotic cell death (Fig. 3): In cells cultured with tet, $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ induced a significant increase of the sub-G1 fraction (representing apoptotic cells). Tet withdrawal further enhanced the proapoptotic effect of $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ . Under the conditions of the experiments, TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ had no effect on cell survival (data not shown). Together, the data suggest STAT1α as an essential mediator of antiproliferative and pro-apoptotic $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ effects in PSCs.
Microarray analysis of gene expression changes also revealed gene products that are down-regulated by $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ , but up-regulated in response to TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ treatment. The list (Table 2) includes two molecules, ET-1 (no. 10) and CTGF (no. 13), that have previously been implicated in autocrine loops regulating PSC function [27,28]. In both cases, microarray data were confirmed by real-time PCR analysis (data not shown). To further study antagonistic regulation of CTGF expression by $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ and TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ , activity of the CTGF promoter in LTC-Tet cells was analysed in luciferase reporter gene assays (Fig. 4). In accordance with previous studies [31], TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ significantly enhanced CTGF promoter activity. In contrast, luciferase expression from the control vector PGL3-Basic was even inhibited by the cytokine. Interestingly, $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ significantly reduced luciferase expression from PGL3-CTGF but not from PGL3-Basic. Together, these data suggest the CTGF promoter as a point of convergence of stimulatory pathways activated by TGF-β1, and inhibitory pathways induced by $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ .
In subsequent experiments, we studied how STAT1α overexpression modulates the effect of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ on CTGF and ET-1 expression in LTC-STAT1 cells. In addition, a known STATinduced $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ target gene, suppressor of cytokine signaling (SOCS)-1 [32], was included in the investigations. Real-time PCR (Fig. 5) analysis indicated an IFN- $\cdot\gamma$ -dependent inhibition of CTGF and ET-1 expression in the presence of tet (no transgene expression), confirming the results of microarray analysis. In addition, a strong induction of SOCS-1 expression was observed. SOCS-1 mRNA levels showed a tendency towards a further increase when $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ stimulation was performed in the absence of tet (induction of STAT1α transgene expression). Furthermore, LTC-STAT1 cells cultured without tet displayed lower levels of CTGF but not of ET-1 mRNA in response to IFN- $\upgamma$ treatment than cells incubated with tet.
## 4. Discussion
In the course of pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer, a transition of quiescent pancreatic stellate cells towards an activated myofibroblastic phenotype is induced by profibrogenic stimuli, including cytokines [1,2,6,7,10]. They are derived from different sources, such as inflammatory cells, injured acinar cells, tumour cells, platelets, and activated PSCs themselves [reviewed in 31]. TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ , the most potent stimulator of ECM synthesis in liver and pancreas, exerts its effects on stellate cells in part through an autocrine loop and the up-regulation of CTGF expression [33]. Another mediator secreted by activated PSCs, ET-1, has been implicated in the stimulation of PSC migration and contractility [28,34].
While extracellular and intracellular induction of PSC activation has been systematically studied in recent years, much less is known about inhibitors and terminators of the activation process. Given that fibrosis is an aggravating factor of pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer, studies on physiological and pharmacological antagonists of PSC activation are of clinical relevance.
Previous investigations with hepatic and pancreatic stellate cells suggested that interferons exert direct inhibitory effects on cell proliferation and collagen synthesis [21,35,36]. In the current study, we have further investigated the molecular basis of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ action in PCSs. A key finding is the observation of a TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ -antagonistic effect of the IFN with respect to the expression of two autocrine PSC effectors, CTGF and ET-1, suggesting a direct interference of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ with signalling pathways involved in PSC activation. To gain further insight into the underlying molecular mechanisms, we studied CTGF promoter activity in LTC-Tet cells. While TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ induced transcription, $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ exerted the opposite effect. Which regulatory elements in the CTGF promoter are targeted by the IFN, remains to be deciphered. Interestingly, lower mRNA levels of CTGF (but not ET-1) in response to $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ stimulation were observed when $\mathrm{STAT1}\upalpha$ was overexpressed. Based on these data, we hypothesise that STAT1 is involved in the inhibition of CTGF expression by $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ .
We have previously reported that the striking differences in the efficiency of individual interferons with respect to the inhibition of PSC growth and collagen synthesis were accompanied by similar differences in their capability to activate STAT1 [21]. Although gene knock-out studies have provided unequivocal evidence of an essential role of STAT1 in type I and type II IFN signalling [37,38], the specific functions of this transcription factor in stellate cells of liver and pancreas are largely unknown. The approach chosen in this study, a tetregulated overexpression of the protein, revealed a direct role of STAT1α in the inhibition of stellate cell growth by $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ . Furthermore, the results implicate $\operatorname{STAT1}\upalpha$ in the mediation of TGF- $_{\cdot\mathrm{\{\beta1}}}$ -antagonistic effects of $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ . Interestingly, a proapoptotic effect of the cytokine was observed even in the absence of transgene expression. In this regard, the analysed immortalised cell clones are different from primary PSCs that were found to be quite resistant against IFN- $\cdot\gamma$ -induced cell death [21]. Nevertheless, the rate of apoptotic cell death further increased when STAT1α-overexpressing cells were exposed to the cytokine. Our data also suggest an additional growthinhibitory effect of $\operatorname{STAT1}\upalpha$ that is independent of IFN- $\gamma$ - induced phosphorylation and activation, because already induction of transgene expression alone was associated with a significantly reduced growth rate.
Recent studies from other laboratories support the hypothesis that inhibition of TGF- $\cdot\upbeta$ signalling represents a key mechanism of the antifibrotic IFN action [39,40]. As another essential mediator of IFN effects in stellate cells, the Y-box protein-1 (YB-1) has been identified. YB-1 activation via the JAK1 signalling pathway has been found to be required and sufficient to confer $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ -dependent activation of the smad7 gene; a negative regulator of TGF- $\cdot\upbeta$ signal transduction [40]. Furthermore, YB-1 is directly involved in the inhibition of collagen gene expression [41]. A strategy of adenovirusmediated YB-1 expression under the control of the COL1A2 enhancer has been successfully used in the mouse model of carbon tetrachloride-induced liver fibrosis to decrease COL1A2 promoter activity and suppress progression of the disease [42]. If YB-1 is involved in the inhibition of ET-1 and CTGF expression by $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ , is currently unknown. In preliminary studies, we have not identified a YB-1 binding element in the CTGF promoter.
In addition to blocking of TGF- $\cdot\upbeta$ action, further mechanisms have been implicated in the inhibitory effects of IFNs on hepatic stellate cells, including attenuation of PDGF signalling pathways, reorganization of intermediate filaments, up-regulation of fatty acid metabolism, and diminished shedding of the proinflammatory cytokine tumour necrosis factor (TNF)- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}$ through a decreased expression of TNF- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\alpha}$ converting enzyme (TACE)/a disintegrin and metalloproteinase 17 (ADAM17) [39]. The contribution of these pathways to IFN effects on activated PSCs remains to be studied.
In conclusion, our data extend the knowledge on the antifibrotic action of interferons by unravelling molecular mechanisms of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ effects on pancreatic stellate cells. Specifically, they suggest STAT1 activation and an inhibition of TFG-β1- enhanced autocrine loops through CTGF and ET-1 as essential elements of $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ action in these cells.
Given the growing body of evidence that IFNs may inhibit fibrosis not only in the special context of virally-induced hepatitis, efficiency of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ should be tested in animal models of pancreatic fibrosis. These studies are also required because not all of our in vitro observations fit the simple picture of IFN$\upgamma$ as a powerful antifibrotic drug: Specifically, attention must be paid to the up-regulation of IL-15 expression by $\mathrm{IFN-\gamma}$ . As previously suggested by our laboratory, IL-15 expression by PSCs contributes to the suppression of spontaneous lymphocyte apoptosis in a co-culture model of both cell types [30]. Consecutively, persistent lymphocyte activation might become an aggravating factor of inflammation and fibrosis. The exact contribution of these divergent IFN- $\upgamma$ effects to the efficiency of the cytokine in vivo remains to be determined.
